/-!
# Groups with n-torsion (ℤ/n)² have ψ(n) cyclic subgroups of order n

Theorem statement:
`AddCommGroup.natCard_isAddCyclic_addSubgroup_eq_dedekindPsi_of_addEquiv_torsionBy`
(`f0b6d6df-0db0-5a8f-baf6-97c9def0bb3a`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `5f2d5eb4-3c9a-5031-88de-79879aca740d`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $n$ be a natural number, assumed nonzero, and let $A$ be an additive commutative group. Suppose
given an isomorphism $e$ of additive groups from $\mathbb Z/n \times \mathbb Z/n$ onto
`Submodule.torsionBy ℤ A n`, the $\mathbb Z$-submodule of $A$ consisting of those $x$ with $n \cdot
x = 0$, i.e. the $n$-torsion subgroup $A[n]$. Then the cardinality (in the sense of `Nat.card`) of
the type of subgroups $H \le A$ satisfying both `IsAddCyclic H`, i.e. $H$ is generated by a single
element, and $\operatorname{card} H = n$, equals [`ModularCurve.dedekindPsi
n`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L201), which is by definition the sum $\sum_{d \mid n,\ d \text{
squarefree}} n/d$; this is the Dedekind value $\psi(n) = n\prod_{p \mid n}(1 + 1/p)$. In particular
the assertion includes that there are only finitely many such subgroups, since otherwise the
left-hand side would be $0$ while $\psi(n) \ge 1$.

This is the group-theoretic count underlying the fact that an elliptic curve whose $n$-torsion is
full has exactly $\psi(n)$ cyclic subgroups of order $n$, i.e. $\psi(n) = \deg$ of the degeneracy
data counting cyclic $n$-isogenies, and hence the number of points of $X_0(n)$ above a given
$j$-invariant in the generic situation. It is used in the project's work on modular curves and
modular polynomials, for instance in the analysis of fibres of the modular polynomial and in
supersingular point counts.

